e check: `to_native(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='strict')`:\n\n```python\ndef to_native(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='strict'):\n    \"\"\" Ensures that a text or binary string is native ... \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(obj, native_str_type):\n        return obj\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), text_type):\n        return to_bytes(obj, encoding, errors)\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), binary_type):\n        return to_text(obj, encoding, errors)\n    else:\n        return obj\n```\n\nHmm, so for non-string objects it returns the object as-is? That would make `\"...\" % to_native(e)` fail with %s formatting... no wait, `%s` on any object works via `str()`. Actually `\"%s\" % obj` calls `str(obj)`. So `to_native(e)` returning the exception object itself is fine for `%s` formatting since `str(HTTPError(...))` works.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me re-check `to_native`:\n\n```python\ndef to_native(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='strict'):\n    \"\"\" Converts any byte string or unicode string to a native string type. \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(obj, native_str_type):\n        return obj\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), text_type):\n        return to_bytes(obj, encoding, errors)\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), binary_type):\n        return to_text(obj, encoding, errors)\n    else:\n        return obj\n```\n\nYes, that's right \u2014 non-strings pass through. OK so exceptions work fine.\n\nFor URLError: `URLError(reason)` \u2014 `str(e)` gives something like `<urlopen error ...>`.\n\nFor SSLValidationError and ConnectionError (from ansible.module_utils.urls): they're simple Exception subclasses, `Exception(\"msg\")`.\n\nNow, the mocked open_url needs to accept all the kwargs. I'll use a MagicMock or a function with `**kwargs`.\n\nTest assertions:\n1. Happy path split_lines=True (default): `lookup.run(['http://example.com'])` returns `['line1', 'line2']` for body `b'line1\\nline2\\n'`. Also assert open_url called once with expected kwargs (e.g., validate_certs=True, timeout=10, etc