f __init__(self):\n        self._options = {}\n        self._task = None\n        self._uuid = uuid.uuid4()\n```\n\nand there's a `plugin_type` property? No. Hmm, in `ansible/plugins/loader.py`, when instantiating:\n\n```python\n        # set load_name/detect based on the loader\n        ...\n        if isinstance(instance, AnsiblePlugin):\n            instance._load_name = name\n```\n\nHmm, and plugin_type... In `AnsiblePlugin`:\n\nActually! I remember now. In ansible-core 2.11+:\n\n```python\nclass AnsiblePlugin(ABC):\n    \"\"\"Base class for all plugins loaded with PluginLoader.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n\n        self._options = {}\n        self._task = None\n        self._uuid = uuid.uuid4()\n```\n\nand in each plugin base class, e.g. `ansible/plugins/callback/__init__.py`:\n\n```python\nclass CallbackBase(AnsiblePlugin):\n    ...\n```\n\nand `set_options` uses `self.plugin_type`... where's plugin_type defined? I believe it's set by the loader:\n\n```python\n            # set plugin_type\n            instance.plugin_type = self.type\n```\n\nHmm, no. OK OK. get_info. Now. For real this time.\n\nLet me also prepare for the possibility that get_info returns limited info. I'll adapt.\n\nLet me make batch 1 calls:\n- get_info(\"LookupModule\")\n- get_info(\"LookupBase\")\n- get_info(\"AnsiblePlugin\")\n- get_info(\"open_url\")\n\nThen batch 2 based on results.\n\nActually, let me think about what names get_info might accept. \"class, function or method name, as in 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C.\" So probably it looks up in the current codebase context (the snippet + ansible). Let me try \"LookupModule\", \"LookupBase\", \"AnsiblePlugin\", \"open_url\", \"LookupBase.set_options\", \"AnsiblePlugin.set_options\", \"AnsiblePlugin.get_option\", \"LookupBase.get_option\".\n\nI'll do batch 1 with 4 calls, then batch 2 with more.\n\nLet me now think ahead about the final test structure, assuming:\n\n- `LookupModule()` can be instantiated directly.\n- `set_options` needs `_load_name` (set manually if missing) and uses the config manager + plugin loader to find DOCUMENTATION for 'url' lookup.\n\nTest file:\n\n```python\n# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"Tests for ansible.plugins.lookup.url.LookupModule.run covering lines 190-224.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.errors import AnsibleError\nfrom ansible.module_utils.six.moves.urllib.error import HTTPError, URLError\nfrom ansible.module_utils.urls import ConnectionError, SSLValidationError\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup import url as url_lookup_plugin\nfrom ansible.plugins.lookup.url import LookupModule\n\n\nclass FakeResponse(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal stand-in for the file-like object returned by open_url.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, body=b''):\n        self._body = body\n\n    def read(self):\n        return self._body\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef lookup():\n    lm = LookupModule()\n    # ``_load_name`` is normally set by the PluginLoader; set it so that\n    # ``set_options`` can resolve the plugin's option definitions.\n    if not hasattr(lm, '_load_name'):\n        lm._load_name = 'url'\n    return lm\n\n\ndef test_run_split_lines_default(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"split_lines defaults to True: response body is split into lines.\"\"\"\n    captured = {}\n\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        captured['term'] = term\n        captured['kwargs'] = kwargs\n        return FakeResponse(b'line1\\nline2\\nline3\\n')\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['line1', 'line2', 'line3']\n    assert captured['term'] == 'http://ansible.example.com/'\n    # options resolved by set_options/get_option are forwarded to open_url\n    assert captured['kwargs']['validate_certs'] is True\n    assert captured['kwargs']['timeout'] == 10\n    assert captured['kwargs']['http_agent'] == 'ansible-httpget'\n    assert 'split_lines' not in captured['kwargs']\n\n\ndef test_run_no_split_lines(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"split_lines=False: whole body is returned as a single string.\"\"\"\n\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        return FakeResponse(b'line1\\nline2')\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'], split_lines=False)\n\n    assert result == ['line1\\nline2']\n\n\ndef test_run_multiple_terms(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    bodies = {\n        'http://one.example.com/': b'a\\nb',\n        'http://two.example.com/': b'c\\nd',\n    }\n    seen = []\n\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        seen.append(term)\n        return FakeResponse(bodies[term])\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    result = lookup.run(['http://one.example.com/', 'http://two.example.com/'])\n\n    assert result == ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n    assert seen == ['http://one.example.com/', 'http://two.example.com/']\n\n\ndef test_run_http_error(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        raise HTTPError(term, 500, 'Internal Server Error', {}, None)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleError) as excinfo:\n        lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert 'Received HTTP error for http://ansible.example.com/' in str(excinfo.value)\n\n\ndef test_run_url_error(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        raise URLError('name resolution failure')\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleError) as excinfo:\n        lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert 'Failed lookup url for http://ansible.example.com/' in str(excinfo.value)\n\n\ndef test_run_ssl_validation_error(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        raise SSLValidationError('certificate verify failed')\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleError) as excinfo:\n        lookup.run(['https://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert \"Error validating the server's certificate for https://ansible.example.com/\" in str(excinfo.value)\n\n\ndef test_run_connection_error(lookup, monkeypatch):\n    def fake_open_url(term, **kwargs):\n        raise ConnectionError('connection refused')\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(url_lookup_plugin, 'open_url', fake_open_url)\n\n    with pytest.raises(AnsibleError) as excinfo:\n        lookup.run(['http://ansible.example.com/'])\n\n    assert 'Error connecting to http://ansible.example.com/' in str(excinfo.value)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 one issue with the HTTPError test: `HTTPError.__init__(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. `str(HTTPError(...))` \u2192 \"HTTP Error 500: Internal Server Error\". And `to_native(e)` \u2014 HTTPError is not a string, so `to_native` returns it as-is, then `\"%s\" % e` \u2192 str(e). Good.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety with `to_native`. Let me double check `to_native` in `ansible/module_utils/_text.py`:\n\n```python\ndef to_native(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='strict'):\n    \"\"\" Converts any byte string or unicode string to a native string type. \"\"\"\n    if isinstance(obj, native_str_type):\n        return obj\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), text_type):\n        return to_bytes(obj, encoding, errors)\n    elif issubclass(type(obj), binary_type):\n        return to_text(obj, encoding, errors)\n    else:\n        return obj\n```\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 `issubclass(type(obj), text_type)` \u2014 for an exception instance, type is HTTPError, not a subclass of text_type. So returns obj. Then `\"%s\" % obj` works. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually in Python 3, `to_native` might be:\n\n```python\ndef to_native(obj, encoding='utf-8', errors='strict'):\n    if issubclass(obj.__class__, text_type):\n        return to_bytes(obj, encoding, errors)\n    elif issub